stated:
>Both EV6 and EV7 have 64K L1 cache for both Data and Instructions. EV6 has 
>off chip L2 cache of varying sizes. EV7 will have an onchip L2 cache of... 
>uh.. 1.75 MB iirc? I'd have to double check but I think that is the 
>ballpark. If your workload fits in L1 cache on an EV68, EV7 probably isn't 
>going to help a lot. But if your app is memory bandwidth hungry, it should 
>scream.

What about 21264E (EV69 with 12.75MB onchip L2) ?

My results would put an 800Mhz Itanium (i2000 workstation) into the current 
#2 position of the second URL and results I've been given for a run on a 1 
Ghz Itanium 2 machine put it solidly in first place by a HUGE margin.
> >
> > Aside from availability, are Alpha processors still priced way above
> > the Pentiums or Itaniums?  That was one big factor in pushing
> > us toward
> > x86 solutions; price/performance ratio.
>
>Pentiums? Yes, probably. Itaniums? I don't think so right now anyway. But 
>with Intel's economy of scale Itanium prices should drop quickly.

I doubt that Itanium will benefit from these same economies.
For example the P4 XEON chips, 1.8GHz, in the regular model, sell for about 
$220
The P4 XEON MP chips, 1.6GHz, with extended on chip cache sell for over $3,800.
The MP is the version with the 512K or 1MB L3 instruction cache, designed 
for 4 way and up MP machines.
see:
http://www.intel.com/products/server/processors/server/xeon_mp/index.htm?iid=ipp_srvr_proc+xeonmp&

Intel was forced to produce these when they were embarrassed by the fact 
that their P3 XEONs, at 900MHz, were outperforming their P4 XEON, 2.4GHz, 
on 4 way MP platforms.

These economies of scale only apply when:
1) There is a scale sufficient to do it
2) When Intel perceives competition on a product.
"Regular" P4 and XEON compete with AMD products.
Large cache XEON costs over 17 times as much as "regular" XEON!
AthlonMP competes with P4 XEON on SMP two way systems.
There are no 4 way Athlons (yet).
This, of course, will change in Q1, 2003 when Opteron based systems ship, 
and I can comfortably predict that XEON MP pricing will drop then!

>Since Compaq .. oops, make that HP :-), is now the only vendor selling 
>Alpha systems, I have no idea really what Alpha prices are (very few 
>people do I'm sure) and you won't be finding any on the open market anyway.

Sorry, but not true.
There are still a few of building and shipping Alpha systems, with one or 
two CPUs.
Compaq ( oops, HP) still don't make the CPUs. Samsung and IBM do. And they 
still sell to others. In selling the Hudson fab to Intel, Digital gave up 
the monopoly on Alpha.

HP makes the only 4 way and up SMP Alphas. In looking at the price of an 
ES4x or a Wildfire it is easy to see that monopoly position!


