>>
>>My experiences with my own apps have shown me that what makes a big
>>difference in performance seems to be the size of the on-chip L1
>>cache.  How big are the caches in each of these processors?
>>    
>>
>
>Both EV6 and EV7 have 64K L1 cache for both Data and Instructions. EV6 has off chip L2 cache of varying sizes. EV7 will have an onchip L2 cache of... uh.. 1.75 MB iirc? I'd have to double check but I think that is the ballpark. If your workload fits in L1 cache on an EV68, EV7 probably isn't going to help a lot. But if your app is memory bandwidth hungry, it should scream.
>
>  
>
>>>I'm only talking about the SETI client here. I'm not sure if any SPEC
>>>numbers have been released for EV7 and I am not aware of what plans
>>>exist in that area (I'm not involved so I don't know).
>>>      
>>>
>>I'd love to see those numbers.
>>    
>>
>
>I promise that as soon as the SETI client is available for download, I'll share exact numbers. Hopefully, that will happen in the next day or two. In the mean time, results for the "TeamLambChop" benchmark are found here:
>http://www.teamlambchop.com/bench/303results.htm 
>and here:
>http://pages.tca.net/roelof/setispy/tlctop200.htm 
>
>My results would put an 800Mhz Itanium (i2000 workstation) into the current #2 position of the second URL and results I've been given for a run on a 1 Ghz Itanium 2 machine put it solidly in first place by a HUGE margin.
>
>  
>
>>Aside from availability, are Alpha processors still priced way above
>>the Pentiums or Itaniums?  That was one big factor in pushing 
>>us toward
>>x86 solutions; price/performance ratio.
>>    
>>
>
>Pentiums? Yes, probably. Itaniums? I don't think so right now anyway. But with Intel's economy of scale Itanium prices should drop quickly.
>
>Since Compaq .. oops, make that HP :-), is now the only vendor selling Alpha systems, I have no idea really what Alpha prices are (very few people do I'm sure) and you won't be finding any on the open market anyway. 
>
>  
>
>>thanks,
>>eyc
>>
>>    
>>

Hi,

I checked out the hp.com website and see that the Itanium 2 zx2000 
workstation retails for nearly $6000 with 900mhz cpu and ide hard disk. 
+ HPUX pre installed. I guess this is fairly reasonable. However how 
much do they cost without HPUX. Anyhows for $6000 do you not get the 
HPUX media or just pre installed. I suppose if you are using linux you 
could pick up your own scsi disk - pretty reasonable off of ebay.com. 
Also what sort of bios do these machines have. Is it anything like srm 
or is it like alpha bios. As for Legosoft TM software I see that windows 
2000 64bit server retails for ~ $3000 on the Compaq website. Cheaper to 
buy a full license of VMS when it becomes available.


cheers,

