On Thu, Aug 08, 2002 at 08:32:14AM +0200, Thomas Elmer wrote:
> 
> After installing a second SCSI controller (Adaptec 2940) I was able to install
> to the 18G drive and everything seemed fine.

This means that after an installation you ended up with an Adaptec
support module, and a corresponding entry in /etc/modules.conf, in
your initrd for a SCSI support.  Other controller needs something else.

> But as the Adaptec-controller was
> not recognised by the console, I was not able to boot this new installation.

You can always boot from a floppy or CD for the first time.  Your
installer is running Linux so you already booted before although into
a ram disk.

> When I reconected the drive to the old LSI controller I was able to boot my
> system from it, but after the kernel was loaded it was not able to mount it and
> paniked.

This is expected in this situation unless you prepared a custom initrd
image which loads a support module for your other controller.  See
above.  'mkinitrd' has options which allow you to add modules you
require even if they will be not picked automatically.  But the fact
that you got a panic means that your firmware and controller do see that
disk or you would not be able to get that far hence a bit of a mystery
why you had problems installing in that configuration in the first
place.

   Michal




