Maurice wrote:
> 
> Correct. For good reasons. Price / performance ratio.
> A number of us feel that given proper performance people are 
> interested.

> And given a competitive price they will buy.

For a little while longer maybe to tide them over until they can do a planned migration. They aren't going to keep buying a platform with no future forever. 

> We are still selling a steady flow of Alphas. There is a demand.

Good for you. 

> However the performance has risen too slowly, and the prices 
> actually went up under API.

Actually, the performance relative to Intel rose too slowly.

> Back when Digital Semiconductor division sold chipsets and EB 
> boards to a 
> number of OEMs the Alpha market was fairly healthy. Unfortunately not 
> enough volume was achieved.
> I blame most of that on marketing droids at Digital who 
> thought that only 
> selling Windows computers counted for anything.

What else would you have sold? Linux wasn't mature back then.
Like it or not, Windows was a VOLUME platform. The problem was the marketing droids couldn't figure out that Volume==Desktop, not Server. And with a lack of native productivity apps (i.e. MS Office), the desktop market was doomed. FX!32 was both a blessing and a curse.

> Selling a 64 bit Alpha running 32 bit Windows was no 
> performance advantage 
> for Alpha..

Actually, it was! For quite a while until Intel came out with the PIII and P4, Alpha had a big performance advantage. Then they caught up. EV6 gained the upper hand again, but Intel kept cranking up the clock speeds regularly. Alpha/NT boxes blew away the competition (including the same machine running Tru64 UNIX) on many many benchmarks for a number of years. Pro/E The Magazine for one.

> By the time Alpha Linux became viable they had already lost 
> so much money 
> on that fiasco, it was too late.

Linux was not accepted in the industry in general yet back in that time frame before Digital sold the Hudson FAB to Intel and Compaq bought Digital. Windows/NT was the only choice really. That was 1998. It is a fairly recent event that Linux has made inroads into enterprise and HTPC functions.

Jeff D
 




