With regards to your message at 09:02 AM 8/8/02, Rich Payne. Where you stated:
>No...I think you're got your names wrong Maurice. Y.J. Kim was a sales
>guy, I think you're talking about Dr. Chin who was the president and CEO
>(and a VP of Samsung itself).
When they were first setting up Alpha Processor Inc. YJ was sent over to 
work on "getting the ball rolling"
He met with the vendors at COMDEX97 and we started placing orders.
At that time the fallback position was for Samsung to build white box 
system as well as boards and CPUs.
They showed of samples of prebuilt barebones UX and BX machines, and had 
just bought rights to the UX/BX design from Deskstation.
They were also fabbing LX boards and CPUs to continue the process and 
market started by Digital Semi on LX and DP264. I believe the CPU boards 
for DP264 came from Samsung, sold through API, but the motherboards were 
purchased from Digital.
  We were also supposed to see other products from this but as DigitalSemi 
was disbanded products like the RX never saw the market.
At the same time Mitsubishi was making the SX CPU and boards under license 
to Digital Semi.

Then, a few months later Dr. Chin was appointed CEO, and took over the reigns.
As I understood it YJ then became sales manager, part time, a he also had 
another position with Samsung at the same time.
Later Samsung continued developing some Alpha board products, such as the 
UP1x00 family, while API took over the barely started development of 
UP2000, and later designed the CS20.

At least that  is what was explained to us at the time.


With our best regards,

