>         telling you otherwise just can't break thru this field.
Well, it's like this:
We never had a situation where Samsung was perceived as screwing us around.
With API we frequently had that situation.
Not with support from guys like you and Rich.
I am talking about cases with sales.
When we are supposedly dealers, and are told to expect a product in 
December, then this keeps getting delayed for months, and in the meantime 
we are finding the product is being sold by them to end users, and then we 
end up with the end users contacting us to get support, I find it disturbing.
> >
> > Correct. For good reasons. Price / performance ratio.
> > A number of us feel that given proper performance people are interested.
> > And given a competitive price they will buy.
>
>         Until Hammer and possibly even Itanic make that
>         all a moot point. Only people with major investments in
>         Alpha who need something to tide them over till the port
>         is done will buy.
Agreed.
People with an investment in Alpha may need to continue that for a while.
I agree that Alpha is a dead duck. I have said so on this forum.
But as long as people need Alphas, and will buy them, we will do our best 
to provide this to them.
We are fully expecting that Hammer based products will be our mainstay in 
this field over the foreseeable future.
Itanium may eventually be there too, but I feel that it is not yet a 
defined target for most of our customers, and, like Alpha before it, will 
take quite a long while to be palatable to the majority.

> > We are still selling a steady flow of Alphas. There is a demand.
> > However the performance has risen too slowly, and the prices
> > actually went
> > up under API.
> > Back when Digital Semiconductor division sold chipsets and EB boards to a
> > number of OEMs the Alpha market was fairly healthy. Unfortunately not
> > enough volume was achieved.
>
>         EV5x boards were not as complicated a design as EV6. EV5x components
>         and chips were cheaper. Intel systems were running about $3k, not 
> under
>         $1k. Alpha performance was 2-3x Intel. The price performance 
> curve was
>         VERY different.

Absolutely.
But the prices Digital was promising us on this stuff in 1996/7 was a lot 
lower than what actually came to market in 98/99

> > I blame most of that on marketing droids at Digital who thought that only
> > selling Windows computers counted for anything.
> > Selling a 64 bit Alpha running 32 bit Windows was no performance
> > advantage
> > for Alpha..
> > By the time Alpha Linux became viable they had already lost so much money
> > on that fiasco, it was too late.
>
>         Alpha Linux wasn't a viable child until about 2001.
Funny, we sold a lot of Alpha Linux machines before then that people were 
very happy to use.
Some of them are still on this mail list.
I agree that timing was bad, and that was the biggest problem.
And the fact that they could not persuade Microsoft to make a true 64 bit 
Windows really did not help either.

But other factors came to play in Digitals marketing for Alpha.
For example the UDB.
An Alpha chip, with crippled memory bandwidth, in a small form factor.
As a desktop machine not a bad idea, and some nifty engineering for the 
time to integrate everything in it.
Trouble is they were charging $4,600 for these!
Then, when they failed to sell, they were stuck with thousands of them in 
warehouses.
Finally they ended up being liquidated for a fraction of cost.
First for around $700 US, and this soon fell to under $100, albeit missing 
some vital parts, like a hard disk.

The main lesson learned is that the transition to 64 bit computing is not easy.
Intel is still learning that with Itanium, which is why we feel that the 
AMD Hammer approach, with intrinsic 32 bit compatibility, is a much better 
idea.
Alpha was designed for high performance computing, but Digital and Compaq 
tried to sell it to commodity computing. With the benefit of hindsight we 
now know that is not a good idea.
BUT, the commodity chipsets for other processors are now coming out with 
the ability to leverage the strengths of an Alpha, and due to economy of 
scale, and newer design technology, may be had at a very low cost.
We feel it is worth exploring.
And _perhaps_ may end up being a product that is desirable.
I am not taking any bets yet, but as Alpha Linux is a lot more mature than 
Itanium or Hammer Linux, there are still some potential advantages for this.

Time will tell..



With our best regards,

