With regards to your message at 12:57 PM 8/9/02, Peter Petrakis. Where you 
stated:

> > I'm sure everyone has seen this already.  Anyone happen to know who that
> > Senior VP at Samsung is?
>
>No though I'm sure he does, You could try asking him. The only thing off on
>his rant is AMD chipsets since IG4 atleast meets the memory performance of
>Tsunami.
>We know the AMD UNI chipsets work though noone AFAIK has tried building an SMP
>Alpha using something like the AMD 760MPX. Sure he would lose the Tru64 side
>of the business though you have to wonder these days how many 'new' customers
>are using that on their cluster farms or et all?
>
>Peter


Stephen (Microway) sell DP264 and UP2000 type systems.
They have an inventory of those parts, including the remainder of APIs 
inventory that they bought when API shut down.
So, I think he has already decided what he wants to sell..
Fair enough, in my books. Good stuff, and they build it well.

I agree with your chipset comments.
The UP1500 has very similar memory bandwidth to a UP2000.
The AMD MPX chipset has better.
The VIA and SiS chipsets for single Athlon improve on that with DDR PC3000 
memory support.
The newest AMD and similar for Hammer type chips has even better bandwidth.

Tsunami is nice.
It is also 4 years ( nearly 5 years) old technology.
It uses PC-100 SDRAM, generally running at an 83MHz clock rate.
On a DP264 they interleave it to double the "pipe width"
On modern AMD related and XEON chipsets they use up to 266MHz clock, DDR to 
up to 533MHz speeds.



