> With regards to your message at 12:57 PM 8/9/02, Peter Petrakis.
> Where you
> stated:
>

> Tsunami is nice.
> It is also 4 years ( nearly 5 years) old technology.
> It uses PC-100 SDRAM, generally running at an 83MHz clock rate.
> On a DP264 they interleave it to double the "pipe width"
> On modern AMD related and XEON chipsets they use up to 266MHz
> clock, DDR to
> up to 533MHz speeds.

DDR memory currently maxes at 200MHz, giving an effective clock of 400MHz,
or 3.2GB/sec on a 64bit bus.  In other words, it ain't 533MHz speeds.

Rambus, OTH, *is* running at 533MHz, and it also sends data on leading and
trailing clock edges (it's ddr, not DDR), but it's only a 16(?)bit bus, so
the end bandwidth comes out the same as comparable DDR memory (which is
64bit, except in exotic circumstances, like Tsunami).

Remember, just like everything else in this industry, it's all bull$#!+.
The DDR community labels their parts PC2100, or some such, which is
representative of bandwidth.  PC2100 = 2.1GB/sec, but it's a 133MHz DDR
part.  The Rambus side labels their stuff based on effective clock rate.
PC800 is a 400MHz part, but it's ddr so it's effectively 800MHz, and good
for 3.2GB/sec (because you *used* to need to install them in pairs).

Anyone confused yet?

I wrote up a nice little chart so I could help myself remember what was
what.  I'll dig it up and post it if anyone wants.
