If it serves as any consolation I have a Miata (PWS433). It is a very 
stable machine. I loaded a default RH7.1 when it first came out. I took 
the machine down a week ago. I forget what the uptime was. I didn't use it 
to provide heavy services or anything. Mostly played around with some 
MySQL/Apache/PHP/PERL stuff on it. It was VERY stable. So it seems that it 
is possible. I have some kind of S3 in it right now (I'm not certain which 
slot). Although I have used it with a permidia2 also.

So, yes it is possible to have a stable Miata, at least in my experience. 
I rather think that it is a very stable platform. I really like mine (not 
that I wouldn't jump at a chance for a newer alpha too ;-)

I use the on board MII 10/100 Ethernet port. I didn't have to do anything 
special to the network driver under RH7.1 that I recall.

hope this helps. At least knowing that it is possible.

James
 On Tue, 13 Aug 2002, Mike Barnes wrote:

> Well, it's not THAT bad, but I'm getting pretty annoyed.
> 
> I wrote to this list previously about some issues with ethernet 
> "freezing" on a PWS 500au. I'm still getting it, but what's REALLY 
> annoying me is that I can't lay my finger on a cause. I only have 
> suspicions. If anyone could tell me if any of these theories make sense, 
> I'd love to hear about it.
> 
> First, let me say that right now it is happily SCPing /proc/kcore from 
> another machine. It's 220 meg in, and running at a decent speed. Last 
> boot, it hung half-way through FTPing the kernel source. It's not 
> consistent with heavy and/or sustained load.
> 
> 
> Theory number one is there's a PCI conflict somewhere. It ALWAYS dies 
> when running X. Locks up to the point that X applications don't respond, 
> even ones running locally (they wouldn't be using eth0 for their 
> connection to the server, surely?) lock up. ifdown eth0/ifup eth0 brings 
> everything back to life for a bit. This makes me think the video card 
> (old S3 Trio32) is screwing up the ethernet interface when it's in a VGA 
> mode. But ... I'm also getting hangs on occasion when in the text 
> console, even with the original Powerstorm card in there.
> 
> Even so, why would a PCI conflict screw up local socket connections 
> (which I'm guessing the X apps are using) as well as ethernet traffic?
> 
> 
> Theory two deals with driver quirks. I've hit problems before with the 
> Tulip driver on a cluster of DS10s we were running for a bit. Those 
> issues don't exist in the Tulip driver included in 2.4.9-32.5, but to be 
> safe I've tried the current one from scyld.com and while it seems to 
> perform better than the stock tulip or tulip_old drivers - it still 
> hangs. Oh, and I dropped a RTL8139-based card into one of the PCI slots 
> which seemed to be doing the same thing. If it's a driver issue, it's at 
> a lower level than the card-specific drivers.
> 
> 
> Can anyone give me suggestions on how to test these theories a little 
> more? Or even just reassure me that a stable Miata is possible? I'd love 
> to get this machine out of my life before I turn 30 (about 12 hours to go).
> 
> 
> Oh, and some random notes that I didn't really include above:
> - Seems to be more likely to lock up with NFS and NIS in use than when 
> using local accounts and local discs.
> 
> - The S3 card only works when inserted into one of the lower 64-bit PCI 
> slots. Corrupts display when starting X if in any other slots.
> 
> - I get machine checks when I try to halt the machine. RH shuts down 
> clean, but I never make it back to SRM. Restarts work fine.
> 
> - There's another Miata I may be able to drop the disk into to test if 
> it's hardware-related. I'm waiting to hear back from its owner if he 
> minds me doing this.
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> I need a hug.
> 
> 
