Thanks to everyone who has replied so far. This is a general update so I 
don't have to write the same thing six times. I know you're all reading 
this list. :)

First, I pulled the drive out of a PWS 433 that wasn't getting a lot of 
use and dropped the drive I've got my RedHat install on into it. 
Verified that the same problems exist. It's not the hardware.

Second, I built a 2.4.18 kernel. I applied the 2.4.9-feral-qlogic-isp 
patch from the RedHat SRPM into it, since the system was using the 
isp_mod module.

(Quick side-question: What's with this module? Does the standard QLogic 
ISP driver have major problems? Is it worth trying it? I kept with it 
just to keep things down to one change at a time.)

On the first boot, I started X and the machine crashed. Hard. Complete 
and total lockup. Nasty.

Third, I swapped another video card into the machine, just to complete 
the "is it the hardware?" test. I popped an S3 Virge card in and didn't 
even get SRM up, but another Trio 64 I had worked OK. Only 1 meg on this 
card so I brought X up at 1024x768x8bpp and still had the same network 
issues. This is still on the 433, so it's a totally different set of 
hardware at this point.



Plans for this evening: relocate to an office on another floor to get 
onto another network switch, and possibly take the bastard home and try 
it on my LAN there if that doesn't work. I'm starting to be very 
suspicious of the network infrastructure, but the PWS433 is running fine 
with Tru64 most of the time. It's not likely to be something as simple 
as two pieces of hardware that just hate each other.

Plan B: Find another hard drive and start the install all over again. 
Pray that the whole thing is down to bit-rot.

Thanks again,
Mike.





