Every once in a while, mostly after a daughter has been
browsing in a dozen windows and running Realplayer at
the same time, the X server will lock up in a strange
way.  Doing Alt-Ctrl-Backspace apparently succeeds in
killing the X server process and all the interactive
user processes involved in the session.  However, the
screen still shows the last contents of the X session,
and switching to another virtual console does not work.

To be more precise, 'ps augx' (from a serial port)
shows no X server or user process from the X session.
The keyboard Num-Lock button toggles the LED, so the
keyboard driver is apparently alive.  The screen shows
the contents from when the X session froze.  Alt-Ctrl-F1
(or any other virtual terminal switching) does not work.
>From a serial port, the rest of the system appears to
be working properly.  Just the screen seems to be ill.

This is on a UP2000, one processor, with an Oxygen VX1
card and a bunch of other PCI cards.  It's running
updated Redhat 7.1 (7.2 CDs just arrived and haven't
been installed) including XFree86-4.1.0-25.

Once the machine gets in this situation, I have not yet
found anything other than reboot that will clear it up
and restore normal function.  I would hope there is some
less severe way to restore proper function.

Any suggestions?

Thanks.

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net




