(Why people clutter email with HTML tags, I don't understand...)

One method I have used for similar purposes is to put in the
installation CD, boot to it just as if starting another
installation.  At the earliest opportunity, hit ALT-CTRL-F2
(it might be another virtual console, but I think F2 is the
one), then use standard unix commands to explore the partition
table (with fdisk), mount partitions (into /tmp/dev or something
like that), look around (with ls, cd, df, du, ...).

If you have some disk space and parititions that can be spared,
another trick is to keep multiple OS installations on the
machine.  That way, you can use one instalation to diagnose and
repair the other.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
