On Mon, Aug 26, 2002 at 07:49:01PM -0700, Ram Viswanadha wrote:
> 
> Disk Druid recognizes disk are labled with BSD label only !!!

Definitely not the case as a number of people got a FAT type partition
table created by Disk Druid when they wanted BSD because there was an
old FAT partition table already present.  Disk Druid may default on
Alpha to a BSD disk label type and does not tell you what kind it
writes, which is nasty and tripped a number of people, but surely it
works with FAT.  Disk Druid will not change a type of an existing
partition table even if a new one did not keep a single partition from
what was there before.

> Created partitions using FDISK and then use Disk Druid to creating the mount
> points.

Ah, so it worked with a non-recognized partition table. :-)

> The installation completes fine. Post-Installation configuration crashes.
> 
> Reboot. Milo does not see any files listed in the partitions..!!!!!
> Does not want to boot from the IDE.
> 
> So as a last resort did
> 
> boot hdd:kernels/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hda3
> 
> The boot completes fine

Means that it does boot from IDE.

> and I can see all files in both IDE and SCSI drives.
> /dev/hda3        ext2           /
> 
> So concluded that the problem may be with Milo...

Kind of.  Most likely you made your / big enough so it has 4K blocks
and/or "sparse superblocks".  Your milo is based on a code of a kernel
which does not know anything about such things so files cannot be found.
A small boot partition formatted with '-b 1024 -O none' would be of a
real help here.  With a small partition these would be defaults.  OTOH
you wrote that you created some FAT partition.  You can put your kernel
and initrd, renaming them to 8+3 scheme, and load them from there.  Once
your kernel is running it will see all your file systems as you noticed
yourself.

  Michal




