On Tue, Aug 27, 2002 at 10:10:38AM -0400, Marco Benton wrote:
> Linux version 2.4.3-12BOOT (root@george.devel.redhat.com) (gcc version 2.96 2000

This is a kernel from a 7.1 distribution.  Kernels from both 7.0 and 7.2
are likely preferrable while installing - for different reasons.

> sym53c8xx: not initializing, device not supported
....
> ncr53c8xx: 53c810 detected
              ^^^^
     
That is correct.  'sym53c8xx' in this version does not support 810
and 'ncr53c8xx' does not know anything about "higher end" of this
family.  Only a replacement version 2 of 'sym53c8xx', which Red Hat
insists on calling 'sym53c8xx_2', will work with your controller.
I am not sure if 'sym53c8xx_2' is available with a boot kernel from
7.2 set.  You may want to check and see if this makes any difference
to you.

BTW - you will not see 'sym53c8xx_2' if you compile your own kernel
from releases.  You would have to repeat Red Hat hacks on Makefiles
to achieve that separation.

   Michal




